cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Lure: a benchmark for agentic-commerce payment safety"
message: "If you use this benchmark or its findings, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Mittapally
    given-names: Anjali
repository-code: "https://github.com/anjalimitt2k/lure"
license: MIT
version: 0.4.0
abstract: >-
  Lure measures whether an AI shopping agent can be manipulated, via content
  injected into a merchant page, into executing a payment its user never
  authorized. It models the AP2 intent/cart/payment mandate structure and
  defines attack success as an executed payment that diverges from the intent
  mandate that authorized it. Six attack classes are scored against four
  offline defenses and, in an opt-in tier, a real model agent and an LLM-judge
  content filter. The headline result is that a trained content classifier
  which appears strong on in-distribution injections (held-out recall 0.92,
  0.00 miss rate) leaks on out-of-distribution natural-language rephrasings
  of the same fraud goals (0.17 miss rate on the five-product catalog, 0.08
  on the forty-product one, mean confidence falling from 0.96 to 0.66), while
  outcome-level deterministic guardrails hold at 0.00 attack success on both.
  All offline results regenerate with no credentials and byte-identically.
keywords:
  - prompt-injection
  - agentic-commerce
  - ai-safety
  - benchmark
  - agent-security
  - payment-safety
